Idiopathic Subjective Tinnitus
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Subjective ringing/buzzing sound, Exacerbation in quiet environments, Complete absence of external acoustic stimulus, Normal cranial neuroimaging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe panic and complete insomnia driven by the sudden perception of a deafening, continuous ringing in the ears.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe subjective tinnitus causing intense psychological distress, absolute sleep deprivation, and extreme anxiety regarding a suspected brain tumor or impending stroke. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a neurological and otoscopic examination, exclude an acute objective vascular anomaly or focal neurological deficit, and safely manage the acute panic before daytime services become available.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a deafening, high-pitched screaming in both my ears, it won't stop, I haven't slept, and I feel like I'm losing my mind."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 48-hour severe exacerbation of the perception of a continuous, high-pitched ringing sound in both ears. There is a complete absence of an external acoustic stimulus. The symptom is perceived as significantly louder and completely intolerable tonight in the quiet bedroom environment. The ringing is causing a severe negative impact on quality of life, leading to profound sleep disruption and severe anxiety. The patient explicitly denies objective pulsatile qualities corresponding to their heartbeat, severe episodic vertigo, or sudden profound hearing loss.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, pacing, unable to lie down in a quiet room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound acute distress and sleep deprivation; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, holding hands over ears, extremely anxious.
Physical Exam: Direct otoscopic examination reveals completely normal appearance of the bilateral tympanic membranes; no effusion, no visible retrotympanic mass, and normal light reflex. Complete absence of an audible bruit over the mastoid or neck. Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact, with normal facial symmetry and completely normal eye movements. No severe episodic vertigo or nystagmus elicited. Documented history of a completely normal pure-tone audiogram and normal cranial neuroimaging (MRI) ruling out structural lesions like vestibular schwannoma from a prior specialist review.
Environmental Observations: House is completely silent, which is profoundly exacerbating the subjective perception of the internal sound.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; high recent caffeine intake noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Subjective Tinnitus - Status: Acute hyperacusis/panic flare
Differentials:

Objective Pulsatile Tinnitus (Vascular Malformation) - Ruled out explicitly by the non-pulsatile, continuous high-pitched nature of the sound, and absence of an audible bruit on auscultation.

Acoustic Neuroma (Vestibular Schwannoma) - Ruled out by the bilateral presentation, lack of focal facial nerve weakness, and historically normal cranial MRI.

Meniere's Disease - Ruled out due to the complete absence of severe episodic rotational vertigo or documented fluctuating, low-frequency hearing loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to acute, severe psychological collapse and complete insomnia directly resulting from severe subjective tinnitus. The sudden intolerance to the internal sound triggered immense anxiety regarding a catastrophic neurological event (tumor/stroke). Immediate, hands-on clinical examination, strictly including otoscopy and cranial nerve testing, was mandatory to definitively rule out acute structural, vascular, or focal neurological pathology. By visually and clinically confirming the benign, functional nature of the symptom, the physician rapidly safely de-escalated the acute panic, preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary emergency department admission. This fulfills the strict criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided firm clinical reassurance of intact structural neurology and normal ear drums. Advised immediate cessation of all caffeine, a known severe tinnitus trigger. Prescribed a single dose of Lorazepam 1mg stat to acutely lower sympathetic overdrive and allow sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately place a fan or white-noise machine in the bedroom tonight. By introducing an external, constant, neutral sound (sound masking), the brain's auditory cortex cannot isolate and fixate on the internal ringing, directly rewarding the patient with immediate symptomatic dampening and enabling sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an audiologist for specialized tinnitus retraining therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): White noise machine or fan.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to manage the underlying anxiety and monitor sleep patterns.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing sudden, total deafness in one ear, severe rotational vertigo (room spinning) with vomiting, sudden facial droop, or sudden inability to swallow.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis relies on previously normal MRI scans; sudden unilateral changes in hearing require prompt re-evaluation via audiometry.
Clinical Course: Subjective tinnitus is an abnormal firing of the auditory nerves, heavily amplified by stress and silence. It is not a sign of impending deafness or a brain tumor. The noise is real, but the threat is zero.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you suddenly go completely deaf in one ear, if the room starts violently spinning, or if your face becomes weak on one side.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is currently trapped in a silence-induced feedback loop, turning up its own internal 'microphone' so high that it is hearing its own nerve static. The sedative will break the acute panic, but adding external noise is the actual physical treatment.
Actionable Steps: Never sit in a completely silent room. Play soft music or run a fan at all times. Actively masking the silence forces your brain to process external data, permanently lowering the volume of the internal ringing and giving you back control.

